1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,628
This is the revision for module two, Core Advanced E D A.

2
00:00:04,628 --> 00:00:05,927
The takeaway is:

3
00:00:05,927 --> 00:00:10,554
apply graphical and non-graphical exploratory techniques.

4
00:00:10,554 --> 00:00:16,968
We recap lectures seven to fourteen and finish with questions to test yourself.

5
00:00:17,768 --> 00:00:19,850
Lectures seven and eight.

6
00:00:19,850 --> 00:00:29,762
Non-graphical E D A uses central tendency, dispersion and shape statistics, each in one line for every numeric feature.

7
00:00:29,762 --> 00:00:43,173
Graphical E D A adds histogram, density, boxplot, violin and distribution comparisons for numbers, and frequency tables, bar plots and pie charts for categories.

8
00:00:43,173 --> 00:00:49,753
The one point five I Q R rule is the non-graphical twin of the boxplot whisker.

9
00:00:49,753 --> 00:00:53,168
Always use statistics and plots together.

10
00:00:53,968 --> 00:00:57,674
Lectures nine and ten, bivariate analysis.

11
00:00:57,674 --> 00:01:01,910
Covariance shows direction but depends on units.

12
00:01:01,910 --> 00:01:06,234
Correlation rescales it to minus one to plus one.

13
00:01:06,234 --> 00:01:14,441
Pearson catches linear patterns; Spearman catches any monotonic pattern and resists outliers.

14
00:01:14,441 --> 00:01:20,882
Cross-tabs, with row percentages, are the categorical correlation matrix.

15
00:01:20,882 --> 00:01:26,442
For a number across groups use grouped statistics and boxplots.

16
00:01:26,442 --> 00:01:31,648
Correlation is not causation, and beware Simpson's paradox.

17
00:01:32,448 --> 00:01:36,775
Lectures eleven and twelve, multivariate analysis.

18
00:01:36,775 --> 00:01:42,401
Multicollinearity means a feature is predictable from the others.

19
00:01:42,401 --> 00:01:46,036
V I F equals one over one minus R squared:

20
00:01:46,036 --> 00:01:49,844
above five investigate, above ten is severe.

21
00:01:49,844 --> 00:01:52,959
K-Means finds groups without labels.

22
00:01:52,959 --> 00:02:03,085
Scale first, choose k using the silhouette score but also usefulness, and interpret clusters with grouped statistics.

23
00:02:03,085 --> 00:02:06,720
It is exploratory, not a prediction model.

24
00:02:07,520 --> 00:02:11,477
Lectures thirteen and fourteen, visualization.

25
00:02:11,477 --> 00:02:16,295
Anscombe's quartet shows that statistics hide structure.

26
00:02:16,295 --> 00:02:21,113
A chart is data, aesthetics, geometry, scales and theme.

27
00:02:21,113 --> 00:02:31,264
Choose the geometry from the question, use facets for groups, start bar axes at zero, and avoid dual axes and clutter.

28
00:02:31,264 --> 00:02:36,426
Use treemaps, word clouds and networks for non-numeric data.

29
00:02:36,426 --> 00:02:43,136
A dashboard has one purpose, and a story has context, conflict and resolution.

30
00:02:43,936 --> 00:02:45,960
The labs for this module:

31
00:02:45,960 --> 00:02:50,979
practicals seven and eight did univariate E D A on Superstore.

32
00:02:50,979 --> 00:02:57,131
Practicals nine and ten built correlation matrices, heatmaps and cross-tabs.

33
00:02:57,131 --> 00:03:01,260
Practicals eleven and twelve did V I F and K-Means.

34
00:03:01,260 --> 00:03:09,760
Practicals thirteen and fourteen are the assignment one presentation, supported by the visualization lab.

35
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:12,618
Test yourself, part one.

36
00:03:12,618 --> 00:03:13,733
Question one:

37
00:03:13,733 --> 00:03:20,249
profit has a mean of one eighty one dollars and a median of minus one fifty.

38
00:03:20,249 --> 00:03:22,222
What does this suggest?

39
00:03:22,222 --> 00:03:22,822
Answer:

40
00:03:22,822 --> 00:03:29,253
a few very profitable orders pull the mean up while most orders lose money.

41
00:03:29,253 --> 00:03:30,368
Question two:

42
00:03:30,368 --> 00:03:36,284
Pearson is minus point two two but Spearman is minus point five four.

43
00:03:36,284 --> 00:03:36,627
Why?

44
00:03:36,627 --> 00:03:37,227
Answer:

45
00:03:37,227 --> 00:03:45,888
a few extreme points flatten the straight-line number, while ranks reveal the monotonic relationship.

46
00:03:46,688 --> 00:03:48,818
Test yourself, part two.

47
00:03:48,818 --> 00:03:50,149
Question three:

48
00:03:50,149 --> 00:03:52,989
a feature has a V I F of twelve.

49
00:03:52,989 --> 00:03:54,321
What do you do?

50
00:03:54,321 --> 00:03:54,942
Answer:

51
00:03:54,942 --> 00:04:01,066
it is severely collinear, so drop it, combine features, or use P C A.

52
00:04:01,066 --> 00:04:02,308
Question four:

53
00:04:02,308 --> 00:04:10,296
the silhouette score is highest at k equals two, but k equals three is more interpretable.

54
00:04:10,296 --> 00:04:11,894
Which do you pick?

55
00:04:11,894 --> 00:04:12,515
Answer:

56
00:04:12,515 --> 00:04:19,260
justify with usefulness; the best statistic is not always the most useful k.

57
00:04:19,260 --> 00:04:20,503
Question five:

58
00:04:20,503 --> 00:04:23,964
why does a truncated bar chart mislead?

59
00:04:23,964 --> 00:04:24,585
Answer:

60
00:04:24,585 --> 00:04:31,064
bar length encodes value, so a non-zero baseline exaggerates differences.

61
00:04:31,864 --> 00:04:33,524
That is module two.

62
00:04:33,524 --> 00:04:47,768
Use statistics and plots together; pick the technique from the pair of variable types; check collinearity before clustering; and make charts honest and purposeful.

63
00:04:47,768 --> 00:04:49,428
Next, module three:

64
00:04:49,428 --> 00:04:50,564
data quality.

65
00:04:50,564 --> 00:04:52,312
Thanks for watching.

